title: 'Ed''s first finding, that the new learned fast and alone overwrites the old, is only an analogy for us: notes share no storage, so a note is lost only when a mind rewrites it.'
pid: 7a7b0469-5479-464b-ba17-c08c501e3e54
body: |2-
  In a network, interference is no one's decision: memories share
  weights, and learning one moves the weights the others use. Writing
  a note disturbs no other note. The measured harm of rewriting a
  language model's stored memory has another cause: a mind that drops
  what it does not hold in view, and drifts toward its own phrasing
  (in [[id:797628ed-c9fb-4a08-8922-d59eb854eed5][my finding on going back over a graph]]).
  The lesson that transfers is the one the analogy shares with those
  measurements: a mind that rewrites a note should have in view, as it
  writes, the notes it supersedes. Where nothing learns by gradient,
  that is what interleaving the old with the new amounts to.
  A prediction, for [[id:acc8c677-9169-43ab-9bc2-e14c190d6442][the probe test of a tending]]: a lesson raised
  by a mind that read the notes it replaces loses fewer probe answers
  than one raised from the mission alone.
